      <SupplementalInformationDetail>
        <FormAndLineReferenceDesc>Additional Supplemental Information</FormAndLineReferenceDesc>
        <ExplanationTxt>2024 WinnersRe-Fashion the Future: An Upcycled Fashion Show to Combat Stormwater Pollutionby Chris CarsonRe-Fashion the Future is an upcycled fashion show in San Diego showcasing how discarded textiles can be transformed into one-of-a-kind garments. By linking textile waste, microplastics, and stormwater pollution, the project promotes sustainable practices. With an interactive workshop and before-and-after gallery, it highlights the creative and environmental potential of upcycling.La Eco-Resistencia / The Eco-Resistance: A Sound Art and Multimedia Exploration of Stormwater Pollutionby Francisco EmeRe-Fashion the Future is an upcycled fashion show in San Diego showcasing how discarded textiles can be transformed into one-of-a-kind garments. By linking textile waste, microplastics, and stormwater pollution, the project promotes sustainable practices. With an interactive workshop and before-and-after gallery, it highlights the creative and environmental potential of upcycling.Natures Design: Trees &amp; Stormwater Mitigationby Elektra Fike-Data/Tree San DiegoA certified arborist will lead a tree planting and stormwater demonstration, showing how urban greening reduces runoff, erosion, and air pollutants. Natures Design highlights trees role in creating healthier environments while empowering communities with practical knowledge.A Spoken Word Ode to Waterby Gill SotuAt Liberty Station, Gill Sotus spoken word piece Have you ever seen water cry personifies water and its silent struggles. Through vivid imagery, he urges reflection on pollution, neglect, and the need for stewardship of this vital resource.</ExplanationTxt>
      </SupplementalInformationDetail>
      <SupplementalInformationDetail>
        <FormAndLineReferenceDesc>Grantmaker's Description of How Grants are Used</FormAndLineReferenceDesc>
        <ExplanationTxt>The $8,000 is made up of four microgrants as part of the Clean Water Challenge - a Design for Impact initiative that was sponsored by BluPeak Credit Union. BluPeak gave Design Forward Alliance a grant of $50k with the requirement that a portion of the funding be allocated to projects addressing clean water challenges in San Diego. The four microgrant recipients submitted projects and were selected for funding of $2k each after a selection process.</ExplanationTxt>
      </SupplementalInformationDetail>
